uint64_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo);

that returns the total number of set bits in both halves (0-128). Provide the function signature and a brief justification of the approach, plus 1-2 quick examples?

questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
This question tests precise bit-manipulation skills across 128-bit data without loops or branches. It validates the ability to split work across halves and combine results safely while demonstrating understanding of efficient parallel counting algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as two 64-bit halves
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining two 64-bit counts to produce a 0-128 result
- Bitmask-based parallel computation

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x3FULL;
}

uint64_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  return popcount64(hi) + popcount64(lo);
}
```

## Examples
- `popcount128(0x8000000000000000ULL, 0x0000000000000001ULL)` returns 2 (bits at positions 127 and 0)
- `popcount128(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL)` returns 128 (all bits set)

The SWAR algorithm processes all 64 bits in parallel using bitmasks to accumulate counts efficiently.

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to unpack bit-packed fields without branches—a common low-level task in networking, codecs, and embedded systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Bitwise masks and shifts** extract subfields from packed integers efficiently
- **No branches** ensures constant-time execution, critical for performance-sensitive code
- **Endianness** is irrelevant when masking at exact bit positions

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t  version;
    uint8_t  type;
    uint16_t length;
    uint8_t  flags;
} Header;

Header extractHeaderFields(uint32_t w) {
    Header h;
    h.version = (w >> 28) & 0xF;  // Bits 31-28
    h.type    = (w >> 24) & 0xF;  // Bits 27-24
    h.length  = (w >> 8)  & 0xFFFF; // Bits 23-8
    h.flags   = w & 0xFF;          // Bits 7-0
    return h;
}
```

Each shift moves the target field to the least significant position, then the mask isolates the relevant bits. This approach is branchless, deterministic, and executes in constant time.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crossing-boundary bit extraction on a 128-bit value with no branching, mirroring real-world crypto/low-level data parsing tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-boundary bit manipulation.
- Mask generation for widths up to 64 without 64-bit overflow.
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves (no loops).

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractBits128(hi, lo, pos, width) {
  const V = (BigInt(hi) << 64n) | BigInt(lo);
  const mask = width === 64 ? (1n << 64n) - 1n : (1n << BigInt(width)) - 1n;
  return (V >> BigInt(pos)) & mask;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply SWAR bit counting to 128-bit data without branches. Also checks splitting 128-bit into halves and combining results efficiently.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR bitcount technique for 64-bit words
- Handling 128-bit data via hi/lo halves
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t v) {
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0f;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101) >> 56;
}
```
Then: hiCount = popcount64(x.hi); loCount = popcount64(x.lo); return hiCount + loCount;

